<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Slice / unit based CPU affinity policy for intel_lpmd.

  This is the systemd-native counterpart to process_cpuset.xml. Instead of
  matching processes by /proc/<pid>/comm, policy is keyed on the systemd
  unit or slice a task already lives in. That identity is assigned by the
  init system and cannot be forged by the process, and applying policy to
  a unit never moves a task out of the cgroup its administrator put it in.

  Tasks covered by an enforceable entry below are NOT touched
  individually: the cgroup does the work, and adding a per-task affinity
  on top could only conflict with it. Tasks not covered by any entry fall
  back to sched_setaffinity() within whatever their current cgroup already
  allows (see <Fallback> below).

  =====================================================================
  What is actually enforceable

  systemd delegates cpu, io, memory and pids into user@<uid>.service, but
  NOT cpuset:

      $ systemctl show user@1000.service -p DelegateControllers
      DelegateControllers=cpu io memory pids

  So AllowedCPUs= is accepted and then silently does nothing on the slices
  owned by the *user* manager (app.slice, background.slice, session.slice).
  Units owned by the *system* manager do enforce it:

      system.slice, user.slice, machine.slice, init.scope   enforced
      system-*.slice and any system service unit             enforced
      user-<uid>.slice, user@<uid>.service, session-N.scope  enforced
      app.slice / background.slice / session.slice           INERT

  Delegating cpuset is a prerequisite, not a cure. Two things have to be
  true before lpmd can set AllowedCPUs= on a unit, and user-manager units
  fail the second one:

    1. the cgroup must enforce it. Fixable by an administrator, with a
       drop-in on user@.service:

           [Service]
           Delegate=cpu cpuset io memory pids

       (Not shipped here: user@.service belongs to the distribution, and
       the change only takes effect for sessions started afterwards.)
       Note cpuset must also reach /user.slice - a controller has to be
       in every ancestor's cgroup.subtree_control to appear in a
       descendant's cgroup.controllers.

    2. lpmd must be able to *ask* for it. Only the manager that owns a
       unit can set its properties, and a user manager is reachable only
       over that user's bus - which authenticates the peer uid and
       accepts nobody else, root included. So a system daemon cannot
       write app.slice, background.slice or session.slice even with
       cpuset fully delegated. lpmd does not try to work around this:
       spawning a PAM login session per transition to borrow the user's
       bus is not something a power daemon should do.

  So <Scope>user</Scope> entries are parsed, resolved and reported, and
  their tasks are handled by <Fallback> - which is why the user entries
  below say Fallback=affinity. They exist so that LIST-SLICES describes
  the whole picture, and so a future systemd that exposes this to the
  system manager needs no config change.

  Run "intel_lpmd_control LIST-SLICES" to see, per entry, whether it is
  enforceable on the running system and what it currently resolves to.

  =====================================================================
  Element reference

  <Unit>
    <Name>              unit or slice name. fnmatch(3) globs allowed
                        ("system-*.slice"). Safe to glob here: unit names
                        are assigned by systemd, not chosen by the task.
    <Scope>             system | user
                        system = owned by the system manager (enforceable)
                        user   = inside user@<uid>.service (see above)
    <Classification>    background | utility | user_initiated |
                        user_interactive | realtime | game_profile_cpu |
                        game_profile_gpu | game_profile_mixed |
                        custom_profile_0..2
                        Resolved through the same class -> core-group
                        mapping process_cpuset.xml uses:
                            realtime         -> Pcores
                            user_interactive -> Pcores + Ecores
                            user_initiated   -> Pcores + Ecores
                            utility          -> Ecores + LPEcores
                            background       -> LPEcores
    <Cores>             OPTIONAL explicit override, taking precedence over
                        <Classification>. Accepts a mix of named groups
                        (ActivePcores / ActiveEcores / ActiveLcores) and
                        literal cpuset lists ("0-3,5").
    <Fallback>          what to do for tasks under this entry when the
                        entry is not enforceable on this system:
                            affinity = sched_setaffinity() per task,
                                       intersected with the cgroup the
                                       task is already in (best effort;
                                       not inherited by later children)
                            none     = leave those tasks alone (default)

  Most specific match wins among entries of the same <Scope>: an entry
  naming a leaf service beats one naming its slice, which beats one
  naming a top-level slice. <Scope> is not a tie-breaker but a filter -
  a task is only ever matched against entries for the manager that owns
  its cgroup, so the same <Name> can appear once per scope and mean
  something different in each.
-->

<SliceCpusetConfig>

    <!--
      Top-level slices are already driven by the global low-power
      transitions (<Mode>0</Mode> writes AllowedCPUs to system.slice,
      user.slice and machine.slice directly), so they are deliberately
      not listed here. An entry for one would do harm rather than
      nothing:

      a slice sitting directly under the root cgroup always passes the
      enforceability probe, because the probe falls back to the parent's
      cgroup.controllers and the root's lists every compiled controller.
      An entry for system.slice would therefore report "the cgroup has
      this one" for every system service at all times, including in
      normal mode when no AllowedCPUs= has been written to it - and the
      per-task path would then skip every daemon, leaving them with no
      policy at all instead of the one their <Process> entry gives them.

      Deeper entries do not have that problem: cpuset only appears in
      system.slice's cgroup.controllers while lpmd has +cpuset on the
      root subtree, so a service entry reports enforceable exactly when
      the cgroup really will enforce it.

      This is why there is no <Scope>system</Scope> background.slice entry
      either, even though a system unit placed there has declared itself
      just as plainly as a user one: it too sits directly under the root
      cgroup. (Fedora 44 ships background.slice as a user unit only, so
      nothing is placed there on a stock system in any case.) Fixing the
      probe to ask "has lpmd actually applied AllowedCPUs= to this unit"
      rather than "could the cgroup enforce it" would make both entries
      safe to add; see TODO.txt.
    -->

    <!-- ============ user session ============ -->

    <!--
      background.slice is where a compliant desktop places work with no
      user interaction and no display output. That placement is the
      signal process_cpuset.xml's name list is trying to approximate.
      Inert today (cpuset not delegated), hence Fallback=affinity.
    -->
    <Unit>
        <Name>background.slice</Name>
        <Scope>user</Scope>
        <Classification>background</Classification>
        <Fallback>affinity</Fallback>
    </Unit>

    <!-- User-launched applications: the default tier for GUI apps. -->
    <Unit>
        <Name>app.slice</Name>
        <Scope>user</Scope>
        <Classification>user_initiated</Classification>
        <Fallback>affinity</Fallback>
    </Unit>

    <!--
      session.slice holds the core session stack (compositor, portals,
      the parts that always drive the visible UI). Deliberately left at
      the interactive tier.
    -->
    <Unit>
        <Name>session.slice</Name>
        <Scope>user</Scope>
        <Classification>user_interactive</Classification>
        <Fallback>none</Fallback>
    </Unit>

    <!-- ============ system services ============ -->

    <!--
      These are enforceable today: the system manager owns them, so
      AllowedCPUs= on the unit takes effect with no task migration and
      no process-name matching. Add or remove entries per distro; an
      entry naming a unit that does not exist is simply reported as
      absent.
    -->

    <!-- Package management and indexing: no interactive latency need. -->
    <Unit>
        <Name>packagekit.service</Name>
        <Scope>system</Scope>
        <Classification>background</Classification>
    </Unit>
    <Unit>
        <Name>dnf5daemon-server.service</Name>
        <Scope>system</Scope>
        <Classification>background</Classification>
    </Unit>
    <Unit>
        <Name>fwupd.service</Name>
        <Scope>system</Scope>
        <Classification>background</Classification>
    </Unit>

    <!-- Crash collection / reporting. -->
    <Unit>
        <Name>abrt*.service</Name>
        <Scope>system</Scope>
        <Classification>background</Classification>
    </Unit>

    <!-- Periodic housekeeping launched into its own slice by systemd. -->
    <Unit>
        <Name>system-systemd\x2dfsck.slice</Name>
        <Scope>system</Scope>
        <Classification>background</Classification>
    </Unit>

    <!--
      Telemetry and monitoring daemons: above background, still not
      interactive.
    -->
    <Unit>
        <Name>smartd.service</Name>
        <Scope>system</Scope>
        <Classification>utility</Classification>
    </Unit>
    <Unit>
        <Name>mcelog.service</Name>
        <Scope>system</Scope>
        <Classification>utility</Classification>
    </Unit>

    <!--
      Audio: latency sensitive. Listed at the realtime tier so that a
      distro which runs pipewire as a system service gets the right
      answer; on a normal desktop pipewire is a *user* unit inside
      session.slice and is covered by that entry instead.
    -->
    <Unit>
        <Name>pipewire.service</Name>
        <Scope>system</Scope>
        <Classification>realtime</Classification>
    </Unit>

    <!--
      Never constrain the display manager stack: it owns the visible UI
      before any user session exists.
    -->
    <Unit>
        <Name>gdm.service</Name>
        <Scope>system</Scope>
        <Classification>user_interactive</Classification>
    </Unit>

</SliceCpusetConfig>
